1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,292
Between 2019 and 2026,

2
00:00:02,342 --> 00:00:07,900
new lambda syntax was proposed on Python's Ideas forum eight times.

3
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:12,431
The history was already there, spread across 745 PEPs,

4
00:00:12,481 --> 00:00:17,550
over a thousand rejected ideas, and over 1,700 forum threads.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,250
Lineage finds it.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,525
Lineage is a company brain for the Python project, built on FalkorDB.

7
00:00:25,575 --> 00:00:28,525
Paste an idea, and it answers from a graph:

8
00:00:28,575 --> 00:00:32,694
who proposed it, what was ruled and by whom, every time it came back,

9
00:00:32,744 --> 00:00:33,600
and who to ask.

10
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:36,700
Null coalescing.

11
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:40,950
PEP 505 proposed it in 2015, and it was deferred.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,399
It came back as PEP 824 in 2026, and in two forum threads,

13
00:00:45,449 --> 00:00:47,700
with the people who took part.

14
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,700
Documents, chats and people, in one graph.

15
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:54,350
Should Python add a switch statement?

16
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,553
Lineage finds PEP 3103, proposed in 2006 and rejected a year later,

17
00:00:59,603 --> 00:01:04,600
and quotes Guido's rejection notice, linked to the exact section.

18
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:06,400
Then it follows the graph.

19
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:11,039
PEP 622 took the idea up again, and was replaced by PEP 634,

20
00:01:11,089 --> 00:01:13,900
the match statement Python has today.

21
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:17,400
Some ideas keep coming back.

22
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:23,050
Keyword arguments in indexing were rejected in 2014, proposed again in 2020,

23
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,800
and rejected by the Steering Council.

24
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,450
Later typing PEPs cite that rejection.

25
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:33,450
Lineage walks those links, and drafts a forum reply built only from what it found.

26
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:36,900
Every answer shows its work:

27
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:41,770
the subgraph the queries walked, each index search, what the cross-encoder kept,

28
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,875
and the exact Cypher that ran, with its parameters.

29
00:01:46,325 --> 00:01:50,634
FalkorDB holds PEPs, rejected ideas, rulings, pull requests,

30
00:01:50,684 --> 00:01:52,450
forum threads and people.

31
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:57,275
Each rejected idea is its own node, linked to the earlier PEP it set aside.

32
00:01:57,325 --> 00:02:00,246
Vector and full-text indexes find candidates,

33
00:02:00,296 --> 00:02:03,680
a local cross-encoder checks they are the same idea,

34
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:05,925
and Cypher traversals do the rest.

35
00:02:05,975 --> 00:02:07,975
PageRank finds who to ask.

36
00:02:08,025 --> 00:02:12,575
The same tools run as an MCP server, so any agent can use them.

37
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:16,325
No language model is required.

38
00:02:16,375 --> 00:02:20,479
When one writes the answer, a guard rejects any PEP number, name,

39
00:02:20,529 --> 00:02:23,100
verdict or quote the graph didn't return.

40
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:27,300
On 98 questions, against vector search over the same data:

41
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:30,450
who decided, 5 of 6, against 1 of 6.

42
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,200
Did the idea come back:

43
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:34,300
3 of 3, against none.

44
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:39,775
Lineage is wrong on 3 of 32 confident matches, and every miss is published.

45
00:02:41,225 --> 00:02:42,575
Lineage.

46
00:02:42,625 --> 00:02:44,975
Has Python already decided this?

47
00:02:45,025 --> 00:02:48,625
Try the live demo, or run it with Docker in about a minute.

48
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:50,325
The links are below.
